Don't know. But especially, given the software market, the "the more the
merrier" seems plain wrong to me; I am more in favour of good standards.
If one standard isn't good enough any more for the particular purpose a
new standard will be developed. Or if we need a new standard to cover new
areas someone will start work on a new standard. Let me stress the parable
a bit more; there are international standards that require 4-5 years for
each iteration, many members in the committees must agree on the decisions.
Reducing the number of members will certainly reduce the development time.

Back to Nethack. I think the biggest challenge is a balanced game; adding
anything possible ("YANI #31654") surely will be contrary to that goal. I
don't think that pushing more people into the process will help building
better thought out software. I don't think that the implementation effort
is the limiting factor; that can be simply compensated by putting 50 times
more people into the process. That's my personal experience. YMMV.

OTOH, I am quite sure that any feature that is not contrary to the goals
of the Nethack development has a chance to be incorporated if made known
to the Devteam; the chance is surely much higher if it's actually code;
not just words.

And if one feels that something *must* be changed there, either feature or
time-to-market, well, initiate your new roguelike standard; if it's worth.

